{{Life in Balisca}}
[[Balisca]] has one of the world's highest rates of cybercrime, ranking # in a 2021 Internet Crime Survey. The most popular form of '''cybercrime in Balisca''' is {{W|cyber fraud}} and is typically achieved via {{W|credit card fraud}}. However, recent decreases in universal credit card usage has seen the expansion of other cybercrimes such as {{W|blackmail}} and {{W|hacking}}. This growth in crime has warranted a government response, with policies specifically addressing the cyberspace being developed. This has necessitated various studies including a cyber security maturity study, and the establishment of the [[Cyberwarfare Forces (Balisca)|Cyberwarfare Division]] of the [[Confederal Armed Forces]]. However the Constitution guarantees the {{W|right to privacy}} and recent laws strengthened {{W|internet privacy}}, which has made it difficult to monitor and catch cybercriminals. 

== Hacking and hacktivism ==
The greater availability of technology and internet services, and the {{W|Computer science education|prolonged government support}} for universal {{W|Technological literacy|technological}} and {{W|digital literacy}} has allowed for the sophistication of hacking techniques in Balisca. Like elsewhere in the world, a significant majority of hackers operate for-profit, mostly via the creation of fake websites aimed at tricking victims into inputting credit card information. Alternatively, Balisca has also seen the creation of pro-justice hacking groups or "{{W|Hacktivism|ethical hackers}}", who have mostly targeted government websites with the goal of exposing corruption or human rights abuses. As of 2022, this is increasingly becoming the more popular form of hacktivism in Balisca, as {{W|digital transformation}} continues around the world.

Depending on who is using the term, hacktivism in Balisca can be a politically motivated technology hack, a constructive form of {{W|anarchic civil disobedience}}, or an undefined {{W|anti-establishment|anti-systemic}} gesture. It can signal {{W|anticapitalist}} or {{W|anarchism|anarchist}} protest; it can denote anti-spam activists, security experts, or open source advocates. Hacktivism by Baliscans against Baliscan individuals or infrastructure is often seen as shadowy due to its anonymity, and is commonly attributed to the work of ideological radicalism and {{W|social withdrawal|outlying members of society}}. In the years before and after the turn of the 21st century, the lack of responsible parties to be held accountable for the social-media attacks performed by hacktivists has created implications in corporate and federal security measures both on and offline. The [[Confederal Congress]] passed a series of laws aimed at deterring "malicious" hacking, defined as that conducted for personal gain, or actions that result in severe financial loss, death or permanent injury. The Constitutional Court, citing [[Article 14 of the Baliscan Basic Laws|Article XIV]], clarified that these protections cannot not extend outside of the Baliscan Confederation. As a result, hacking conducted against targets outside of the Baliscan Confederation for "ethical" reasons is fully legal.

Some to the left and right of the mainstream Baliscan progressive left describing themselves as hacktivists have taken to defacing websites for political reasons, such as attacking and defacing websites of foreign governments, corporations and those who oppose their ideology. Others have argued forcefully against definitions of hacktivism that include web defacements or denial-of-service attacks.

While sizable numbers self-described hacktivists have engaged in DoS attacks, some Baliscans assert that DoS attacks are an attack on free speech and that they have unintended consequences. DoS attacks waste resources and they can lead to a "DoS war" that nobody will win. Both the [[Ministry of Science and Innovation (Balisca)|Ministry of Science]] and the [[Constitutional Court (Balisca)|Constitutional Court]] determined in 2022 that DoS attacks did not constitute an attack on free speech. [[Michi Fumizuki]], the incumbent Minister of Digitalization, asserted that DDoS is a legitimate form of protest speech in situations that are reasonably limited in time, place and manner.

